  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 1210 AM CDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nA few lingering showers are possible on Tuesday, mainly in our \neastern counties. Mostly cloudy skies will become partly cloudy, and \nexpect above normal afternoon highs in the upper 70s and lower 80s.\nSimilar story for Wednesday with mostly cloudy skies becoming partly \ncloudy. Temperatures will be a little warmer with highs 80-85 \ndegrees.   \n\nSeeing indications for our area to have a more significant rain\nevent Thursday into Friday. A more substantial shortwave trough \nis progged to move northeast across our area during that time, and\nthe remnants of what is currently tropical cyclone Vance (off the\nwest coast of Mexico) could get pulled northeast and incorporated\ninto this trough. Our area will have enhanced lift with a \nsouthern stream upper level jet and divergence. With anomalously \nhigh precipitable water values (for final week of October) progged\nto be 1.4 to 1.6 inches, heavy rainfall will be possible. \nEnsemble model guidance points to this as well, will need to \nmonitor the potential for flooding. \n\nWith the cloud cover and rain, temperatures will be cooler Thursday \nand Friday. As confidence increases in this scenario unfolding, \nanticipate that downward adjustments to current forecast highs will \nbe needed. At this time, looking like highs should be in the 70s \nThursday and mid 60s to mid 70s Friday. Lower rain chances will \ncontinue into Saturday with the possibility of an additional \ndisturbance entering the area in southwest flow aloft. Mostly to \npartly cloudy skies are expected over the weekend with a warming \ntrend in temperatures. Highs should be in the mid to upper 70s for \nmost of the area Saturday, and upper 70s to lower 80s for Sunday.",
